{
  "slug": "find-a-financial-advisor/recently-sold-a-business",
  "status": "draft",
  "page_type": "niche-specialty",
  "meta_title": "Recently Sold a Business: Organize the Next Decisions | Valora",
  "meta_description": "Organize received proceeds, conditional payments and remaining commitments after a business sale. Compare planning scope, custody, costs and next questions.",
  "h1": "Recently sold a business: organize the facts before choosing investments",
  "body_html": "<p>Start with signed terms, cash actually received and obligations still open, then choose professional help for the decisions those facts create. A post-sale planning engagement should explain what is confirmed, what needs tax or legal review and what remains uncertain before presenting an investment proposal.</p>\n<p>This guide is for US owners after closing. Pre-deal valuation and negotiation belong to a different assignment. It is educational, not individual investment, tax or legal advice. Valora is not a registered investment adviser and is not arranging advisor matches or introductions at this time. No specialist roster or availability is offered here.</p>\n<aside class=\"worksheet key-takeaways\"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Start with signed documents and cash actually received, keeping conditional payments and remaining commitments separate.</li><li>Assign tax and legal questions to qualified professionals before treating sale proceeds as available household resources.</li><li>Define the household-planning assignment before comparing investment proposals or moving accounts.</li><li>Compare written scope, custody, full costs and conflicts, and verify any professional independently.</li></ul></aside><h2>Begin with a closing record, not a headline number</h2>\n<p>Ask for a reconciled record of received proceeds and remaining commitments. The amount discussed during negotiation is not enough to establish what your household now owns or can spend.</p>\n<p>Collect the signed sale agreement, closing statement, relevant schedules and evidence of receipts. Identify the recipient of each payment: a business entity, you personally or another party. Ask counsel and the tax professional how the actual transaction and remaining obligations should be understood. A large account balance alone does not establish that all of it is available for personal goals.</p>\n<p>Keep a dated list of adjustments or questions still open. Which items were finalized at closing, which remain subject to later reconciliation and who is responsible for explaining them? If two documents use different labels or amounts, record the discrepancy rather than choosing the larger figure. Ask the appropriate professional to resolve it.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/close-or-sell-your-business\">SBA sale-of-business guidance</a> emphasizes documenting assets, liabilities, adjustments and relevant terms in the agreement. After closing, your task is to work from the completed records, not recreate negotiations from memory. This guide does not interpret your agreement or declare that closing ended every obligation.</p>\n<h2>Separate received cash from conditional proceeds</h2>\n<p>Put money already received in a different category from escrow, earnouts, deferred amounts and retained ownership. Treat uncertain future receipts as open questions, not as a guaranteed balance for spending or investment.</p>\n<p>For each component, identify the document, current status and professional who can explain it. A scheduled payment and a payment dependent on a future condition need different questions. Ask counsel what governs payment, release, possible reductions and any continuing responsibilities. Do not replace that review with a general description of how earnouts usually work.</p>\n<p>Ask the planning professional to show household scenarios with uncertain amounts clearly separated. What could you do using confirmed resources alone? Which goals depend on future receipts? What changes if a receipt is delayed or smaller than expected? These are planning questions, not instructions to spend or invest a particular amount.</p>\n<p>Retained equity needs its own record of ownership and restrictions. A valuation estimate does not turn it into liquid savings. Ask what documents control rights, obligations and any ability to sell. Do not assume a later exit will happen because the original transaction included an optimistic illustration.</p>\n<section class=\"worksheet\"><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width:600px)\" srcset=\"/assets/recently-sold-a-business/diagram-1-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"/assets/recently-sold-a-business/diagram-1.svg\" alt=\"A post-close inventory separates confirmed facts, estimates and unresolved questions instead of combining them.\" loading=\"lazy\"></picture><figcaption>A post-close inventory separates confirmed facts, estimates and unresolved questions instead of combining them. Illustrative framework.</figcaption></figure><h2>Use a post-close fact, estimate and open-question inventory</h2>\n<p>Give every important item a document, status, responsible professional and next question. Keeping estimates visibly separate from confirmed facts helps prevent an early investment proposal from treating uncertain money as available.</p>\n<p>This is a private organizing worksheet, not a tax calculator or legal checklist declaring your sale complete. Fill it using the actual records. An unknown should stay unknown until someone qualified resolves it.</p>\n<article class=\"worksheet-row\"><h3>Received proceeds</h3>\n<p>Document: closing statement and evidence of receipt. Confirmed fact: amount, date and recipient that records establish. Still uncertain: unexplained adjustments or transfers. Professional responsible: the person engaged to reconcile records. Next question: which amounts belong to the household versus the entity?</p>\n<p class=\"write-line\">Private note: ______________________</p></article><article class=\"worksheet-row\"><h3>Escrow or holdback</h3>\n<p>Document: controlling agreement or escrow record. Confirmed fact: balance and terms verified by the relevant professional. Still uncertain: release, reductions or disputes. Professional responsible: counsel for interpretation. Next question: what must happen before money becomes available, and how will status changes be reported?</p>\n<p class=\"write-line\">Private note: ______________________</p></article><article class=\"worksheet-row\"><h3>Earnout or deferred payment</h3>\n<p>Document: payment provisions and supporting schedules. Confirmed fact: what has actually been received. Still uncertain: conditions, timing and collectability questions. Professional responsible: counsel and tax professional for their respective scopes. Next question: which household goals currently depend on amounts not yet received?</p>\n<p class=\"write-line\">Private note: ______________________</p></article><article class=\"worksheet-row\"><h3>Tax treatment and payment work</h3>\n<p>Document: sale records, basis information and tax correspondence. Confirmed fact: reviewed assumptions and payments already made. Still uncertain: treatment, reporting, state consequences or amounts needing review. Professional responsible: qualified tax professional. Next question: what must be checked before treating the remaining cash as available?</p>\n<p class=\"write-line\">Private note: ______________________</p></article><article class=\"worksheet-row\"><h3>Continuing commitments</h3>\n<p>Document: relevant guarantees, agreements, employment terms or other actual records. Confirmed fact: obligations the relevant professional has identified. Still uncertain: interpretation or settlement questions. Professional responsible: counsel or other engaged specialist. Next question: who monitors what remains, and how does the household plan account for it?</p>\n<p class=\"write-line\">Private note: ______________________</p></article><article class=\"worksheet-row\"><h3>Household resources and goals</h3>\n<p>Document: existing account statements and a household inventory. Confirmed fact: resources, income and commitments supported by records. Still uncertain: future spending, work plans or major goals. Professional responsible: planning professional within the agreed engagement. Next question: what decision comes first, and what information could change it?</p>\n<p class=\"write-line\">Private note: ______________________</p></article><article class=\"worksheet-row\"><h3>Investment engagement</h3>\n<p>Document: proposed agreement, disclosures and custody information. Confirmed fact: written services, authority and costs. Still uncertain: exclusions, total costs or reporting responsibilities. Professional responsible: proposed adviser and your independent review. Next question: what must be settled before signing or transferring assets?</p>\n<p>Add a checked date and next review question to each row. Do not enter account numbers, passwords, full agreements or financial statements in a public form. The worksheet is visible text you can use privately; it does not collect or store your information.</p><p class=\"write-line\">Private note: ______________________</p></article>\n</section><aside class=\"worksheet content-cta\"><h2>Want help applying this to your own numbers?</h2><p>Tell us what you are trying to figure out. Valora reviews inquiries and emails next steps; we are not arranging advisor matches or introductions. Do not submit confidential financial documents or account details.</p><a href=\"/find-your-advisor/\" data-content-event=\"content_cta_click\" data-content-page=\"recently-sold-a-business\" data-content-placement=\"mid\" data-content-destination=\"inquiry\">Talk it through</a></aside><figure><picture><source media=\"(max-width:600px)\" srcset=\"/assets/recently-sold-a-business/diagram-2-mobile.svg\"><img src=\"/assets/recently-sold-a-business/diagram-2.svg\" alt=\"Check transaction records, assign tax and legal review, then define the household planning assignment.\" loading=\"lazy\"></picture><figcaption>Check transaction records, assign tax and legal review, then define the household planning assignment. Illustrative framework.</figcaption></figure><h2>Resolve tax questions from the actual transaction</h2>\n<p>Ask the tax professional to connect completed sale records to reporting and payment questions. Do not assume the whole transaction receives one capital-gains treatment, or that a generic percentage establishes an appropriate tax reserve.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/sale-of-a-business\">IRS sale-of-business guidance</a> explains that an asset sale can involve separately treated assets, with inventory producing ordinary income or loss and other assets treated differently. It also distinguishes ownership-interest transactions. The signed deal and underlying records matter more than a broad label such as \u201cbusiness exit.\u201d</p>\n<p>Ask what is confirmed about basis, allocation, seller identity and prior records, and which questions remain open. A planning professional can incorporate a reviewed estimate into household scenarios, but that does not replace tax preparation or transaction-specific analysis. State and entity questions should not disappear merely because a federal summary is available.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p537\">IRS Publication 537</a> explains installment-sale conditions and exceptions. Inventory does not qualify, and depreciation recapture can require recognition in the year of sale even without a corresponding installment receipt that year. It also addresses contingent payments and escrow. Ask what applies to your completed transaction rather than assuming all deferred money means deferred tax.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/estimated-taxes\">IRS estimated-tax guidance</a> says taxes are paid during the year through withholding or estimated payments, and insufficient or late payments may produce penalties. Ask a tax professional to review your actual payment timing and circumstances. This page supplies no personal deadline, safe-harbor conclusion or reserve percentage.</p>\n<h2>Define the household assignment before a portfolio proposal</h2>\n<p>Choose planning work that connects confirmed resources with the life decisions following the sale. Ask for a written first deliverable before committing to ongoing investment management.</p>\n<p>The assignment might be organizing cash needs, comparing work scenarios, checking how existing accounts relate to new resources or establishing what remains uncertain. Write the decision in one sentence. \u201cHelp me organize what is available and what commitments remain before I change my work\u201d is more specific than \u201cmanage the proceeds.\u201d</p>\n<p>Bring existing wealth into the picture rather than reviewing only the new receipt. Identify current investments, debt, recurring income, spending commitments and near-term goals. Do not assume every account should move to the new adviser. Ask which information is necessary to evaluate the household without making an immediate transfer recommendation.</p>\n<p>Compare project planning with an ongoing engagement. What is included now, what begins later and what happens if you delay an investment decision? Ask how the adviser coordinates with tax and legal professionals and whether the communication requires your authorization. A useful plan identifies the work and its limits; it does not imply the adviser interprets every clause or prepares your returns.</p>\n<h2>Compare investment scope, custody and costs</h2>\n<p>Request the proposed agreement and applicable disclosures before transferring assets. Confirm what the adviser would do, who holds the assets, who may authorize trades and what you would pay across the relationship.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional/investment-advisers\">Investor.gov's adviser guidance</a> explains that services and fees depend on the contract and recommends reviewing the agreement, relationship summary and Form ADV brochure. <a href=\"https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins/relationship-summaries-form-crs-or-form-adv-part-3-investor-bulletin\">Form CRS guidance</a> describes information about services, investment authority, costs and conflicts. Confirm which disclosures apply to the proposed firm and relationship.</p>\n<p>Separate planning charges, adviser charges, product expenses, custody or account costs and possible transaction costs. Ask how fees change if you keep assets elsewhere or choose a limited engagement. No fee benchmark is supplied here; compare written proposals for the same work rather than assume a familiar percentage is the whole cost.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-73\">SEC custody bulletin</a> encourages investors to understand custody arrangements and compare adviser information with custodian statements. Ask who sends statements and how you would independently contact the custodian. This is a diligence question, not a guarantee against loss or a claim that every planning professional is covered by the same custody rules.</p>\n<h2>Protect against rushed product decisions</h2>\n<p>Be cautious about pressure to invest immediately, promised returns or a proposal made before the documents and goals are understood. A sale does not make an unsolicited investment pitch more trustworthy.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.investor.gov/protect-your-investments/fraud/how-avoid-fraud/red-flags-investment-fraud-checklist\">Investor.gov's fraud checklist</a> identifies unlicensed sellers, risk-free claims, guaranteed returns and pressure to invest now as warning signs. Use those signals to pause and verify, not to label every unfamiliar proposal a scam without evidence.</p>\n<p>Check the individual and firm through official resources and obtain contact details independently. Ask how a proposed product relates to the planning assignment, what can be lost, what restrictions apply and how the seller is paid. A complex explanation or an impressive title is not a substitute for understandable documents.</p>\n<p>Separate decisions with real reviewed timing requirements from sales urgency. Ask which tax or legal question has a documented time constraint and which investment choice can wait for review. This page does not say delaying every action is safe; it says the responsible professional should identify the actual reason for a deadline.</p>\n<h2>A fictional owner after closing</h2>\n<p>Imagine a fictional owner who has received one payment while an escrow balance and an earnout remain unresolved. The first meeting should organize those facts and identify who must check them, not invest a combined headline amount as if it were already available.</p>\n<p>The owner also has existing savings and is considering a work change. A proposed planning assignment compares goals using confirmed resources, marks conditional receipts separately and incorporates tax assumptions only after review. Counsel handles the agreement questions within scope. The adviser explains investment authority, custody, fees and exclusions before any transfer.</p>\n<p>This is a selection exercise, not a client case or promised result. Ask a candidate to show what they would need, what they would leave unresolved and what they could deliver first. A candidate who identifies a missing document may be answering more usefully than one who offers a confident allocation immediately.</p>\n<h2>Prepare your first post-sale meeting</h2>\n<p>Bring a document inventory and a short list of decisions, then agree on secure sharing before supplying records. Do not upload closing statements, tax returns or account credentials to a public inquiry form.</p>\n<p>Ask: Which proceeds are confirmed? What remains conditional? Who reviews tax treatment and payment questions? What commitments survive the closing? What planning work can happen without moving assets? Who holds and reports on investments? What are the full costs and conflicts? What will the first deliverable answer?</p>\n<p>Record the professionals responsible and the next unresolved question. A meeting should leave you able to distinguish legal review, tax review, household planning and investment management rather than treating them as one vague service.</p>\n<p>For broad ownership context, read the <a href=\"/financial-advisor-for-business-owners/\" data-content-event=\"content_internal_link_click\" data-content-page=\"recently-sold-a-business\" data-content-placement=\"body\">business-owner educational guide</a>. The <a href=\"/find-a-financial-advisor/business-owner/\" data-content-event=\"content_internal_link_click\" data-content-page=\"recently-sold-a-business\" data-content-placement=\"body\">owner advisor-selection guide</a> covers general scope comparison. Pre-deal negotiation and valuation are separate from this post-close inventory; return to the actual signed terms when deciding what you now need.</p><h2>Conclusion: organize the facts before choosing investments</h2><p>After closing, choose help that separates confirmed resources from estimates and open commitments. Signed terms and reviewed tax assumptions belong at the start of the household plan, not after an investment proposal.</p><p>Use the inventory privately to identify the document, responsible professional and next question for each item. Ask for a written first deliverable, then compare investment scope and costs without assuming uncertain proceeds are already available.</p>",
  "faqs": [
    {
      "q": "What should I organize first after selling a business?",
      "a": "Start with signed terms, the closing statement, evidence of received payments and continuing commitments. Identify the recipient of each payment and separate confirmed resources from unresolved amounts. Ask the relevant professionals to reconcile discrepancies before treating a headline sale price as available household cash."
    },
    {
      "q": "Should I count an earnout or escrow as available cash?",
      "a": "Keep it separate from money received. Ask counsel to explain the actual payment or release conditions and remaining obligations. Have the planning professional show what household decisions depend on uncertain receipts, rather than assume the maximum amount will arrive."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does a completed business sale always produce only capital gains?",
      "a": "No. IRS sale-of-business guidance describes different treatment for different assets and distinguishes ownership-interest transactions. The completed agreement, records and seller circumstances need tax review. A broad business-exit label does not establish one tax rate or treatment."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can tax be due before all deferred proceeds arrive?",
      "a": "It can. IRS Publication 537 describes installment-sale conditions and exceptions, including inventory and depreciation recapture. Ask a tax professional to review recognition and payment questions using the actual transaction, rather than assume all deferred receipts mean deferred tax."
    },
    {
      "q": "What should I compare before moving proceeds to an adviser?",
      "a": "Compare written planning and investment scope, authority, custody arrangements, total costs, conflicts and exclusions. Review applicable disclosures and verify the firm and person. Ask what planning work can happen without moving assets and how records can be checked independently."
    },
    {
      "q": "What investment-pitch warning signs should I watch for?",
      "a": "Investor.gov identifies pressure to invest now, risk-free claims, guaranteed returns and unlicensed sellers as warning signs. Pause and verify the person, firm and proposal through independent sources. A recent business sale does not make an unsolicited offer more trustworthy."
    }
  ],
